  "query_preview": "..\nClear this morning with the exception of some low clouds or\npatchy fog for higher valleys east of Tucson. Current RH is\n90-98% for areas along the I-10 corridor, so its not out of the\nquestion for patchy fog. Any fog will quickly dissipate after\nsunrise. \n\nDry and pleasant today with temps near normal. A Pacific storm \nsystem moves onshore Tuesday with a lead shortwave and \nassociated frontal bndry passing through the region during the\nafternoon. Heaviest precip well north of the area, but isolated\nto scattered showers still forecast Tuesday morning through \nTuesday evening. Overall rain amounts should be fairly light \nwith the forecast ranging from a few hundredths to around two \ntenths of an inch, with Pinal and northern Graham-Greenlee \nseeing the upper end of the spectrum. Temperatures will cool a \nfew degrees and surface winds will turn out of the southwest and\nincrease to 15-20 mph with afternoon gusts to 30 mph.\n\nAnother trough passage will likely occur Friday into Saturday,\nthough current ensembles favor keeping precipitation well to the\nnorth again. Temperatures bounce up to near normal ahead of this\ntrough on Thursday then fall below normal Friday into the\nweekend.",
